## Level 2 · Pitfalls {.rq-level}


::: {.rq-item data-id="w6-04" data-world="w6" data-type="mcq" data-diff="3" data-tags="model-building,confounding" data-hash="cspm4t.eioim7" data-show="VGhlIHNjcmVlbiBkaXNjYXJkZWQgYSBjb25mb3VuZGVyIHRoYXQgY3J1ZGUgdGVzdHMgaGlk" data-explain="Q29uZm91bmRpbmcgY2FuIG1hc2sgYW4gYXNzb2NpYXRpb24gaW4gY3J1ZGUgYW5hbHlzaXMuIFNlbGVjdCBjb3ZhcmlhdGVzIGZyb20gYSBjYXVzYWwgZGlhZ3JhbSBvciB0aGUgbGl0ZXJhdHVyZSBhbmQgY2hlY2sgdGhlIGNoYW5nZSBpbiB0aGUgZXhwb3N1cmUgZXN0aW1hdGUsIG5vdCB1bml2YXJpYWJsZSBwLXZhbHVlcy4=" data-hints="WyJBIHZhcmlhYmxlIGNhbiBsb29rIHVucmVsYXRlZCBhbG9uZSBhbmQgbWF0dGVyIG9uY2Ugb3RoZXJzIGFyZSBpbiB0aGUgbW9kZWwuIl0="}
A thesis screens covariates and keeps only those with univariable p < 0.2. Physical activity has a univariable p near 1 but changes the age-adjusted OR for the exposure by 20%. What went wrong?

- The p-value threshold was applied to the wrong test
- Physical activity should have been entered as a mediator
- The screen discarded a confounder that crude tests hid
